"""
The how-to-use-the-app guide that Help mode answers from (routers/help.py).

Keep this in step with the app. It is the ONLY thing Help may answer from, so a screen that has changed and isn't
updated here becomes a wrong answer given with confidence. Add a line here whenever a release moves, renames or adds
something a person would look for. Anything not covered should make Help say it isn't sure and point to the email.
"""

from utils.sugar import tracking_on

SUPPORT_EMAIL = "hello@causalme.com"

APP_GUIDE = """
THE GAP: HOW THE APP WORKS

WHAT IT IS
The Gap finds which of a person's habits seem to move their body's numbers (sleep, HRV, resting heart rate and so on). It uses the person's own data from a wearable or Apple Health plus what they log. It is general information about their own data. It is not medical advice and does not diagnose anything.

THE FIVE TABS (along the bottom): Home, Your Health, Insights, Ask Gappy, Settings.

HOME
- "Make The Gap yours" card (near the top of Home): a ring showing how much of the personal set-up is done, with a list of what is left (connect Apple Health, connect a wearable the person said they own, a first daily check-in, weight and height, a health goal, a first meal, food and water targets, a workout, notifications, the calendar, a home address). It only lists things the person can actually do on their phone (a wearable only if they said they have one; notifications or the calendar are left off if the iPhone has been told no). Tapping an item opens the place where it is done. It disappears at 100%, and can be hidden with "Hide this for now" or turned off and on in Settings, under Preferences, "Profile checklist".
- A short tour (six cards, with Next and Skip tour) appears the first time someone reaches Home after setting up. Settings, under Questions & answers, has "Take the app tour again".
- Quick Entry button: opens the Journal screen, where the daily check-in and the "What just happened?" box live.
- Today's one thing: a single suggestion that unlocks after the day's check-in is saved.
- Heads-up cards: short coach-style messages about today compared with the person's usual (a big step day, a short night, water or protein running behind). Tap the X to hide one. Water and protein cards go away by themselves once the person has logged enough.
- The strip under the greeting. If this phone is the device chosen for steps, it says LIVE and shows steps, calories burned and heart rate read straight from Apple Health on the phone, refreshed every 20 seconds while Home is open. If another device is chosen for steps (for example Whoop) it instead says LATEST and the device's name, shows that device's most recent saved steps (and calories), and says when it was last checked ("today · checked 12 min ago", or "from Tuesday" if there's nothing for today yet). It deliberately doesn't say live for a device like Whoop: Whoop's numbers only reach The Gap after the strap has synced to the Whoop app and The Gap has pulled them (about hourly, or on a refresh), and a wrist counts steps differently from a phone, so the two numbers differ and Whoop's day runs from waking to waking, not midnight to midnight.
- Pull down to refresh: pulling the Home page down re-reads this phone's Apple Health data, reloads everything on the page and checks the heads-ups. The heavier re-sync with connected devices only happens if the last one was more than about ten minutes ago, otherwise the page just reloads. It's limited to three pulls a minute; after that it says "You're up to date. Give it a minute before refreshing again." The refresh button at the top right does a full re-sync of connected devices on demand, and the server won't start a second sync for the same person within about 15 seconds of the last or while one is running ("Already up to date"). Whoop and similar devices still update only as often as they send their data.
- Key readings (for example from Whoop or Apple Health), tappable for history. Three hexagon gauges (for example sleep, recovery and HRV) can be tapped to switch between them. Under the gauges is "Your reading": one or two sentences about that exact number, written by AI, usually comparing it with the person's own usual. It is refreshed at most once an hour (sooner only if the number itself changes). If it can't be written (AI turned off in Settings, no connection, or the trial has ended) a general line about why the reading matters is shown instead. "Understand this better" opens the full view: first what the reading is in plain words (for example sleep is the total time spent asleep, not counting time lying awake in bed), then the full personal reading, then the typical range.
- Order of Home, top to bottom: the greeting and live numbers, heads-up cards, new discoveries, Quick Entry, today's one thing, the three reading gauges, then (only when there are any) the weekly review and research cards picked for the person, then "Your progress", then the research feed.
- "Your progress" is one card below the readings that is closed by default; tap it to open or close it (the app remembers). Closed, it shows a one-line summary such as "5-day streak · 3 confirmed findings · workout planned today". Open, it holds the streak and Weekly recap link, how many days of data have been collected, Closest to a new finding, In progress (things being tried), Goals teaser, Weekly recap, Workouts teaser, and (for anyone who has trained in the last six weeks) a one-line "2 of 4 workouts this week" with a small ring, which opens Workouts. The weekly target is the one on the Workouts screen ring.
- Cards from the "Check this out" feed: short summaries of published research, with a link to the study. The X hides a card.
- An icon at the top of Home opens History, which shows any past day (day summary and Quick Entries).

JOURNAL SCREEN (opened from Quick Entry on Home)
- Daily check-in: tap the header ("Daily check-in") to open it. It asks about alcohol, caffeine, stress (1 to 10), energy drinks, cigarettes, gambling, work, arguments, travel, screen time and coffees. It opens on Yesterday before midday and on Today after. A past day can be picked and edited. Saving the same day again replaces it. "Choose what to track" at the bottom of the check-in switches categories on or off. A category that is switched off is left out of the analysis (it does not count as zero). Supplements (magnesium, melatonin, ashwagandha, omega-3) are off until switched on there; if on, they only let the app compare the person's own days with and without. It never tells anyone to take anything.
- "What just happened?" box: type a note about something that just happened (a drink of water, a cigarette, an argument, a coffee) and tap Log. Simple things are sorted automatically into water, caffeine, alcohol, cigarettes, weight or saved foods, and every note is also kept as a note. Quick prompts appear above the box when it is empty.
- A mic button next to the box (in the newest version of the app) lets the person talk instead of typing: tap it, speak, tap it again to stop, check the words, then tap Log. The first time, the phone asks to allow the microphone and speech recognition. If there is no mic button, the app needs updating from TestFlight or the App Store.
- A small list button on the left of the box opens "Anything happen today you might have forgotten?": twenty short things that often affect sleep, recovery and HRV, such as feeling more stressed or emotional than usual, an argument, being around people the person doesn't get along with, feeling bullied or walked over, feeling sick, travelling, a very late night, a boss saying good job, a really nice meal out, good news or a great time with friends. Tick any that apply, tap Done, and the sentences are added to the box to edit before tapping Log. Nothing is logged until Log is tapped. These are written so the app can pick them up as stress, conflict, illness, travel or routine change, a big win, or mood, which the engine then compares with sleep and HRV over time. It needs many days of notes before it can say anything.
- At the bottom of the Journal screen, "How Gappy read your notes" can be opened to see, day by day, how the app understood the notes (for example a good day or a tough day, and whether it noticed stress, travel, feeling unwell, conflict or a big win).
- The same thing can be done by voice with Siri when the app is closed (see Voice logging).

YOUR HEALTH TAB
- Add food screen: at the top is "Adding to" with the four meals (Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner, Snack) and the time eaten (tap the time to change it; it starts at the usual time for that meal). Under that is the search box and two buttons, "Scan a barcode" and "Add your own". With nothing typed there are three tabs: Recent (foods logged before), My meals (saved meals and "regular" foods) and Restaurants (takeaway and restaurant chains). Tapping a food opens its page: the amount (a stepper, a typed number, and quick choices of half, 1, 1 and a half, 2 or 3 servings, or 50 to 250 g when grams are available), a Servings or Grams switch, the calories and macros for that amount, and a fixed "Add to dinner" button at the bottom. An info icon next to "Per serving" opens "What's a serving?": labels give numbers per 100 g so foods can be compared fairly; a serving is a typical portion; when a food is only known per 100 g, one serving is 100 g and Grams should be used for what was actually eaten; raw and cooked weights differ (dry pasta is roughly two to two and a half times heavier cooked); oils, butter and sauces are small but high in calories (a tablespoon of oil is about 160 kcal in Australia, where a tablespoon is 20 ml). It also gives rough sizes for 100 g of common foods.
- Build a meal: on the Add food screen, the "Build a meal" button opens a screen for home-cooked meals. The person names the meal, says how many serves it makes (a big pot of spag bol might make 4), and adds ingredients by searching, each by grams or by servings. Totals update as they go: one serve, and the whole recipe. "Often forgotten" buttons add typical amounts of olive oil (1 tablespoon), butter (10 g) and cheese (30 g), which are small to look at and big in calories. They can optionally enter the cooked weight of the whole pot. Saving puts the meal under My meals. Tapping a saved meal opens "How much did you have?": half a serve, 1, 1 and a half, 2 serves, or a quarter, third or half of the pot, or (if the pot was weighed) the grams on their plate, with the calories and macros shown before logging. A built meal can be changed later from that same screen with "Edit ingredients". Each ingredient is saved as its share of one serve, so logging the meal once adds one serve. The numbers are those of the foods picked, so they are estimates; weighing ingredients gives the best result. A built meal can also be logged by saying its name, including from Siri and with phrases made in Your voice, your command. Editing a built meal needs a database update (phase26) that is run once; before then a built meal still saves and logs, but can't be reopened.
- Your Health screen order: the day's headings and goals, workouts, water, food notes to log, then "Today's food" (one heading that can be folded away by tapping it, the choice is remembered; it holds Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner and Snack; the small (i) beside it opens "What you've eaten today": the day's calories against the goal, how the energy splits between protein, carbs and fat, a table by meal, and the biggest contributors; sugar and fibre are not recorded yet), then the weight card (the last thing to log in a day), then recipe ideas. The weight card has one weight box for the day being viewed; earlier weights are in a drop-down called "Previous days" that is closed until tapped.
- Describe it: at the top of the Build a meal screen, "Describe it instead" lets the person say (mic button, in builds that have it; it keeps listening through pauses until the mic is tapped again, and joins the words together if the phone ends a stretch by itself) or type how they make a meal, for example "Spag bol for 4: 500 g mince, a jar of pasta sauce, 200 g spaghetti, an onion, a tablespoon of olive oil and cheese on top", then tap "Build it for me". AI reads the words into an ingredient list with amounts (using Australian measures: a tablespoon is 20 ml); the calories and macros come from the same USDA food database as search, not from the AI. The AI's own rough numbers are used only when the database has no sensible match, and those lines are tagged "estimate"; lines where the person didn't say an amount and the AI assumed a typical one are tagged "guess". The list goes into the builder for the person to check and change, and nothing is saved or logged until they tap Save. It follows the other AI features: it needs AI features switched on in Settings, an active subscription or free month, and uses the monthly AI allowance. It doesn't add ingredients that weren't mentioned, such as oil or salt.
- Nutrition: log food (search, scan a barcode, takeaway and restaurant items, "My regulars" saved foods and saved meals, or add your own), and water. Daily goals for calories, protein and water can be set. Tapping an entry lets you edit its servings, meal or time.
- Daily goals (the pencil or "Daily goals" on Your Health): calories, protein and water. Under protein and water the app shows a suggested range with a "Why this?" and a "Use" button. The ranges come from published guidance and the person's weight and goal; they are general guidance, not medical or dietitian advice. Suggestions can be switched off in Food preferences ("Don't suggest targets for me"), which is meant for pregnancy, breastfeeding, a medical reason to limit protein or fluids, or preferring to set your own. The app never suggests calorie targets.
- A saved or built meal that is logged shows as ONE line in its meal (for example "Spag bol, 11 ingredients, 1,137 kcal") with a small arrow: tap it to open the ingredients, each of which can be edited or removed on its own. The bin on the meal's line removes the whole meal. (Meals logged before this change are joined up the same way when they were saved in the same moment.)
- Weight card: log weight; body fat and weight can also arrive from Apple Health.
- Workouts / Training: plan a workout and check it off, start a workout, see the week's schedule, and ideas for meals and training.
- Reminders: a reminders button on Your Health, and Settings under Notifications & voice, let the person set water, meal and workout reminders, a bump for yesterday's check-in if it is not done yet and another for today's, and quiet hours. Each is off until switched on, has its own times, and is dropped once that check-in is saved. These are notifications from the phone itself.
- "Food notes to log": foods the person mentioned in a note that are not saved yet.

- Personalise for me: the first time the Nutrition or the Training page on Your Health is opened (and again after a week if the person taps Not now), a card offers a few quick questions, one at a time. Nutrition: the person's food aim (lose fat, maintain, gain muscle), eating style and allergies, weight and height, then a protein target, a water target, an optional calorie goal and, when sugar tracking is on, an optional sugar limit. Training: main goal, equipment, and workouts per week. Every step can be skipped and nothing is saved until the last step. Suggested ranges are general guidance and are hidden for anyone who switched suggestions off in Food preferences. The app never suggests a calorie number. At the end it points at the Goals button, where goals are changed later. It can be run again from Goals, then "Personalise for me". The profile checklist on Home also links to it.

WORKOUTS
- Start a workout: Browse by type (7 types), For you (suggestions from the person's own habits), My templates, or start from scratch and pick exercises. There are about 43 ready-made workouts that can be edited.
- During a workout: log sets, reps and weights. For-time and AMRAP workouts use one clock for the whole workout, with an Ideal split (a time per round) or a Custom split (as many sections as wanted).
- In Training preferences the person can optionally choose which plans to show first: Women's, Men's, or No preference. It only changes the order (women's picks lead with glutes, legs and strength plans; men's with upper-body strength). Every plan stays available. The Start a workout screen also asks once if they haven't chosen.
- Progression: when a workout is started from a template or a program, each movement's weights are filled in from the person's own past lifts, with a blue line under the movement's name saying why (for example "You hit every rep last time (60 kg x 8, 8, 8). Try 62.5 kg (+2.5)."). The rule: once every rep is hit at a weight, add a little weight next time; miss reps once and repeat the weight; miss them twice and drop about 10% to rebuild. If recovery looks low that day, the weight isn't pushed. All of it can be changed.
- Rest timer: in Actual mode, finishing a set starts a rest countdown at the bottom of the workout screen, with -15s, +15s and Skip. The phone buzzes when the rest is over, and a quiet notification says so if the app is in the background (if notifications are allowed). "Rest timer: Auto" under the Planned/Actual switch changes it: Auto (about 2.5 minutes after sets of 5 reps or fewer, 90 seconds up to 12 reps, 1 minute above that), 1 minute, 90 seconds, 2 minutes, 3 minutes, or Off. There's no rest after the last set, for timed movements, or in for-time and AMRAP workouts. The choice follows the person to a new phone.
- Suggested weights also appear when repeating a past workout or starting one of the person's own saved templates.
- Finishing a workout that sets a new personal record (a heavier weight, or a better estimated one-rep max) shows a "New personal record" message. The Workouts screen also shows Personal bests (strongest lifts, ranked by estimated one-rep max), Muscle balance (sets per muscle group over the last 7 days, and groups that have gone quiet), and a "This week" ring: workouts done against a weekly target, with the days ticked and the number of weeks in a row the target was met. The target starts from the suggested range for the person's goal (or 3), and the + and - under the ring change it. The target and the active program are kept on the person's account, so they follow them to a new phone.
- Programs: Start a workout has a Programs list (for example Two days a week, Full body three days, Glutes and strength, Four-day strength split). Starting one puts a Your program card on the Workouts screen showing the week, the sessions done, and a Start button for the next session. Sessions count as done when a workout with the same name is completed that week. Longer programs have a lighter final week at about 90% of the usual weights. Stop program is on the card.
- Any workout can be saved as "my own template". Workouts can be planned for another day, and history shows past ones.

INSIGHTS TAB
- Two views: Findings and Improve.
- Filter what you see (Findings view): two rows of chips, "What it affects" (for example HRV or sleep) and "Because of" (the habit, for example alcohol or caffeine), each with a count. Tapping one narrows the confirmed findings, the early findings, the early patterns being watched and the tests still running to just those that match; "Showing X of Y" and Clear appear while a filter is on. With a filter on, every match is listed instead of the first few.
- Findings: confirmed findings (a pattern that passed the app's checks against chance) and "still checking" cards, also called early signals (faint hints that may be chance; the card says STILL CHECKING). New confirmed findings arrive sealed ("Sealed discovery"): tap to open it. Tap a finding for its details, a chart, and "Related research" (published studies on the same subject, general information about groups of people, not about the person). A study may carry a line saying whether it points the same way as the person's own data or the other way; the person's own data always comes first. Share is available for confirmed findings only.
- Inside a finding's details: "How big is this?" (the finding next to the person's own usual range for that reading, with a plain word such as small, modest, clear or large), "Your days, side by side" (the reading on days with and without the habit, or on lower, middle and higher days, showing the average, the typical range, and the best and worst day, plus a scatter of their days for amounts), "Your last 45 days" (a timeline with the habit days highlighted), and "How we got this number" (numbered steps with the real day counts, what was accounted for, the likely range, and the check for chance). Findings saved before this was added show it after the next update of their data.
- Most tests need about three weeks of days with both the habit and the reading recorded. Until then the tab shows "Day X of Y" progress.
- "Try this": starts tracking a suggestion, and the app follows up about a week later with whether it seemed to help.
- Improve: today's focus, what the person is trying, findings ready to try, goals, food and workout cards, and short videos.
- "Big changes in your life" card (above the patterns): the person can add a lost or new job, a move, a relationship change, a new baby, an injury or illness, or a loss of someone close, with a date and an optional short note. Once there are enough days on each side, the app shows what their HRV, sleep, resting heart rate and steps did around it. This is a description, not a cause. Events can be deleted. When a note the person logs mentions one (for example losing a job), the app offers to add it, and nothing is added unless they tap Add. Gappy can refer to them (type and date only, never the note).
- Patterns card: best and worst days, and "What tends to come together in your days" (for example, on the days a person logged an argument, how many also had no lunch logged). These are same-day counts, not causes, and they only appear after several days of the event.
- Findings about a supplement the person logged are only a description of their own days. There is no Try this or improvement plan for them.

ASK GAPPY TAB
- Two modes at the top: "My data" (Gappy answers questions about the person's own findings and numbers) and "How to use the app" (this help). Help is free and does not use the AI allowance.
- Gappy never recommends a supplement or medicine, never gives doses, and does not give medical advice.
- Each subscription has a monthly AI allowance. The free month has a smaller one and up to 10 Gappy questions a day. When it is used up the Gappy screen says when it resets and, during the free month, offers "Go Pro now".

GOALS
- When setting a goal for steps, sleep or workout days, the app shows a common range and a realistic first step based on the person's recent average, with a "Use" button.
- Open Goals from Home (the goals teaser). Set a goal on a reading (for example sleep per night, steps, HRV), with a target. "Find research for my goals" adds research cards to Home. Achieved goals are listed.

SUPPORT AND SAFETY
- Settings has a "Need help now?" row at the top (always visible). It opens a screen of free Australian support lines, each one tap to call: 000 (danger right now), Lifeline 13 11 14 (24 hours, also text 0477 13 11 14), Suicide Call Back Service 1300 659 467, Beyond Blue 1300 22 4636, 13YARN 13 92 76, Kids Helpline 1800 55 1800, Gambling Help Online 1800 858 858, and the National Alcohol and Other Drug Hotline 1800 250 015. Outside Australia: findahelpline.com. The What just happened screen and the Gappy tab have a small "Struggling? Free support is here" link, and the check-in shows a quiet support line when alcohol (5 or more drinks), gambling or other substances are logged.
- If a note in What just happened, or a question to Gappy, contains wording that suggests self-harm, suicide or a very dark place, the app shows "A note from Gappy" with the numbers (a pop-up for notes; in the Gappy chat, a caring reply with call buttons). It is a simple phrase check, so it can miss things or fire when it was not meant that way; "I'm okay" closes it. It needs no AI, no subscription and uses no AI allowance, nothing about it is stored, and the note is kept as normal. The Gap is not a crisis service and can't give medical advice. If someone tells Help they are struggling, give these numbers and say 000 for danger right now.

SETTINGS TAB (five sections that open when tapped)
- Connections: Data sources (choose which device is the main one for each kind of reading), Apple Health, Device Calendar, "Add a Google or other calendar" (opens iPhone Settings to add the account), and the connections for Whoop, Oura, Strava, Withings and Polar. A background-sync switch for Apple Health is here too. Voice logging has its own place now (see Notifications & voice).
- Questions & answers: a list of common questions, and "Take the app tour again".
- Previously confirmed: a confirmed finding stays confirmed for up to two weeks after the data stops backing it. After that it moves to a "Previously confirmed" section on the Insights tab (kept, with the dates it was confirmed, not deleted). The "Check old findings" button there re-tests everything against the latest data; a finding only returns to the confirmed list if it is confirmed again. It never forces one back.
- Notifications & voice: everything that reaches the person or listens to them, each switched off until they choose. Reminders on the phone (water, meals, workouts, a bump for an unfinished check-in, quiet hours); notifications from The Gap (new findings, the weekly recap, follow-ups on things being tried, connection problems); the coach's heads-ups; the well-done pop-ups (with a link to start a cool-down stretch); voice logging (Siri, Action Button, Back Tap); "Your voice, your command"; and a check that notifications work.
- Preferences: the switches that change how the app behaves: background sync for Apple Health and the profile checklist switch.
- Policies & Procedures: How your data is used, AI features (a switch to turn AI features off), Manage subscription, See The Gap Pro plans, Weekly recap, Privacy Policy, Terms of Service, Credits, export your data, and delete your data or account.
- About You: name, height, weight and similar details.
- Sign out is in Settings.

CONNECTING DEVICES
- Settings, then Connections. Apple Health, Whoop, Oura, Strava, Withings and Polar can be connected. Hume body scales can send weight and body fat through Apple Health (turn on Apple Health syncing in the Hume app). Garmin and Fitbit cannot be connected directly yet.
- Whoop, Oura and the other connected services sync about every hour. Apple Health updates when the app is opened and about hourly in the background while the phone is in use. Background updates stop if the app is swiped away, Low Power Mode is on, or Background App Refresh is off.
- With more than one device, Data sources lets the person choose which one is the main one for each reading.

VOICE LOGGING
- Say "Hey Siri, log to The Gap" (also "Log The Gap", "Add to The Gap", "Add a log to The Gap"), then what happened, for example "just had a cigarette" or "500 ml of water".
- It is switched on in Settings, then Notifications & voice, then Voice logging. That section also has a guide for putting it on the Action Button or Back Tap: it uses a two-step shortcut (Dictate Text, then Log to The Gap) so the button listens instead of asking the person to type. In the shortcut, tap the "What happened?" box and pick the Dictated Text bubble above the keyboard; do not type the words.
- If it logs the words "Dictated text", the shortcut has those words typed in instead of the Dictated Text bubble.
- Your voice, your command: Settings, then Notifications & voice, has "Your voice, your command: set your own phrases for logging". The person can add up to 30 of their own phrases, each logging one thing: an amount of water (50 to 3,000 ml), a number of coffees, alcoholic drinks, energy drinks or cigarettes (1 to 10), or a number of servings of one of their saved foods or meals. For example "big bottle" logs 750 ml of water. Saying a number first multiplies it ("two big bottles" is 1,500 ml). Phrases work everywhere words are sorted: Siri and the Shortcuts action, and typing or talking in Quick Entry. A person's own phrase wins over the built-in rules, and the built-in rules then skip that kind of thing in the same sentence so nothing is counted twice. The screen also has a Try it box that shows what a sentence would log without saving anything, and a list of what already works with no setup (a glass of water is 250 ml, a bottle 500 ml, a flat white is a coffee, two beers is two alcoholic drinks, and so on). Phrases must be plain words (2 to 40 letters, up to six words) and not so common they'd catch everyday sentences. It is switched on for everyone and needs no new version of the app. Everything said is also kept as a note.

IF THE APP GOES WRONG
- If a screen ever hits a problem the app shows "Something went wrong" with a Reload The Gap button instead of closing, and sends a short crash report (the error, the app version and the iPhone's system version, but no health data, journal text or names) so it can be fixed. Tapping Reload restarts the app, and the person's data is safe. If the app closes by itself, opening it again is fine; if it keeps happening, email hello@causalme.com with what they were doing and roughly when.

WELL-DONE POP-UPS
- A few moments get a short pop-up with a tick. They are used sparingly and each happens once: (1) "Good job!" the first time a day's daily check-in is saved (for today it also says Today's one thing is unlocked on Home); fixing a check-in later doesn't repeat it. (2) "Workout complete!" the moment the last set of a whole workout is marked done in the Actual view, with an offer to "Start a 5-minute stretch" or "Maybe later". For-time and AMRAP workouts get the same offer after they are saved. (3) "Weekly goal reached!" the first time a saved workout takes the person to their weekly workout target in a week. (4) "Water goal reached!" the first time each day the water total crosses the daily water goal.
- The pop-ups can be switched off in Settings (Notifications & voice section, "Well-done pop-ups"). It is kept on that phone. Off hides the ticks and the stretch offer after a workout; the same place has a "Start a 5-minute cool-down stretch" link so the stretch is still one tap away.
- The cool-down stretch is a short guided screen: seven gentle moves (breathing, calf, front of thigh, back of thigh, hip flexor, chest and shoulders, child's pose), about five minutes, with a countdown, a buzz at each change and halfway through the moves done on both sides, plus pause and skip. It is general information, not medical advice, and nothing is logged or analysed from it. If the phone locks, it carries on from the clock.

UNDO
- After logging water, a food or recipe, a saved meal, or a Quick Entry, a small bar appears at the bottom of the screen for about eight seconds saying what was logged, with an Undo button. Tapping Undo removes exactly what that one log added. For a Quick Entry it removes everything the sentence did (the note, and any water, food, check-in counts or weight it logged) and puts the words back in the box so a typo can be fixed and logged again. The bar shows one log at a time: logging something new replaces it.
- Not covered by the bar: weight logged on the weight card (it has its own remove), the daily check-in (it has Delete), and finishing a workout. Anything can still be removed later from Your Health or the Journal.

NOTIFICATIONS
- The Gap sends: new-discovery notifications, heads-ups (at most three a day, none during quiet hours, water and protein ones only in the afternoon and evening), follow-ups on things being tried, the weekly recap, and any reminders the person set. Every one of these except the coach's heads-ups starts switched off, and the person turns on the ones they want in Settings, under Notifications & voice, where they also choose reminder times. Turn reminders off there or in the Reminders sheet. iPhone Settings, then Notifications, then The Gap controls everything.
- Setup when the app is first opened has four steps: a welcome, what the person wears, Apple Health, and wearables to connect. It does not ask for notifications or the calendar. Those are asked later, when they have a clear use:
  - Notifications: under the first heads-up on Home (or Your Health) a card asks "Want these on your lock screen?" with Turn on notifications and Not now. Not now hides it for a week. Tapping Turn on notifications shows the iPhone's own pop-up, which the iPhone only ever shows once. The "Check" row in Settings also asks if it has never been asked. If someone said no on the pop-up, they can turn notifications on in iPhone Settings, then Notifications, then The Gap.
  - Calendar: on the Insights tab, under "more on the way", a card appears when patterns that need the calendar (meetings and busy days against sleep and recovery) are waiting. It uses only how many events there were and for how long, never titles or who is in them. Not now hides it for two weeks. It can also be turned on any time in Settings, under Device Calendar.
  - A reminder before the free month ends is only delivered if notifications are on.
- If notifications are not arriving: check iPhone Settings, then Notifications, then The Gap is allowed; check Focus modes and quiet hours.

SUBSCRIPTION
- The Gap Pro starts with a free month, then renews at the price shown when the person started. Cancel any time in iPhone Settings, then the person's name, then Subscriptions, at least 24 hours before it renews. Billing is run by Apple. A refund request goes to Apple.
- During the free month, "Go Pro now" on the Gappy screen ends the free month early and starts paying straight away with the full AI allowance.

DATA AND PRIVACY
- Data is used to find patterns for the person and is never sold. There are no ads. Some features send the details they need to an AI service to write summaries, never a name or email. AI features can be turned off in Settings, under Policies & Procedures.
- Export or delete data: Settings, then Policies & Procedures. To fully remove Apple Health access, also turn it off in iPhone Settings, then Privacy & Security, then Health.

COMMON PROBLEMS
- "I have no insights yet": patterns need days of data. About three weeks of days with both the habit and the reading. The daily check-in matters most. Wear the device overnight. Missed days are fine.
- "My numbers are not updating": check the connection in Settings, then Connections; open the app to refresh Apple Health; check Background App Refresh and Low Power Mode.
- "Nothing happens when I tap Log": check the internet connection and try again.
- A check-in edit is saved by tapping Update.
"""

# Sugar is only described once it is switched on (SUGAR_TRACKING on the server), so the guide never offers something the app doesn't have yet.
SUGAR_GUIDE = """
SUGAR
- Foods found by search or barcode show their sugar (total sugars, in grams) beside calories, protein, carbs and fat. Each food is saved with its sugar. Foods that have no sugar figure (some takeaway items, recipes built by hand, foods typed in by hand) are shown without one: they are never counted as zero.
- Your Health shows a Sugar box beside Protein, Carbs and Fat, with a dash if a food that day had no figure. The (i) beside Today's food gives the detail: sugar so far, its share of the day's energy, and how many foods had a figure.
- A daily sugar limit can be set in Daily goals ("Sugar limit (g)"). The World Health Organization suggests free sugars stay under 10% of energy (about 50 g on 2,000 kcal), and under 5% (about 25 g) is better. The Gap counts all sugars in food, including those in fruit and milk, so the number runs higher than free sugars. It is a gauge, not a rule, and not medical advice.
- Once there are several weeks of days where every food had a sugar figure, the engine starts testing whether more sugar on a day goes with a different HRV the next morning or a different amount of sleep that night. Days where some food had no sugar figure are skipped, not guessed. The results appear in Insights like any other pattern.
"""

if tracking_on():
    APP_GUIDE = APP_GUIDE + SUGAR_GUIDE
